  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 237 PM CDT Fri Oct 16 2026\n\nGusty northwest winds will gradually diminish this afternoon as\nCanadian high pressure builds into the region. Clear skies & \nlight winds tonight will allow temperatures to quickly fall into\nthe 30s, with widespread sub-freezing temperatures expected \novernight. A Freeze Warning is in effect overnight through 9 AM\nSaturday across the entire area, with the exception of the inner\ncore of the Twin Cities metro. Southwesterly winds could pick up\nearlier in the night across western Minnesota & keep\ntemperatures a few degrees above freezing, but HRRR & AIFS\nforecasts still suggests freezing temperatures are likely here\nalbeit closer to midnight instead of Saturday morning. Tonight's\nfreeze will effectively end the growing season across much of \nthe region, with our next shot a widespread freeze likely not \ncoming until November, so take care to protect remaining \nsensitive outdoor vegetation should be protected.\n\nThe cold spell will be brief as the high pressure departs to the\neast and gusty southwest winds develop through Saturday morning.\nWind gusts increase to 25-30 mph by the afternoon along with\ntemperatures warm into the low 60s across western Minnesota &\nupper 50s elsewhere. This combination of gusty winds, warmer\ntemperatures, & afternoon humidity values approaching 30% will \nlead to elevated fire weather conditions. The greatest concern \nwill be across western Minnesota, where the warmest & windiest \nconditions are expected along with drier fuels that missed \nThursdays soaking rain.\n\nTemperatures continue warming into next week as upper-level\nridging strengthens across the central CONUS. Highs in the low \nto mid 70s are likely by the end of the week, around 10-15 \ndegrees above normal for mid-October. Dry weather looks to \ndominate the period with any appreciable rain not unlikely \nuntil next weekend.",
